Your `alarm()` handler never runs, and the timer that does run is lost when the object is evicted. Here are the problems in order of impact, then a corrected version. I wrote this from the durable-objects skill and my own knowledge of the alarm API. I didn't fetch the current Cloudflare docs or run the code.

1. **Nothing ever calls `setAlarm()`, so `alarm()` never fires.** A Durable Object only gets an alarm event after `this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(...)` sets one.
2. **`setTimeout` can't do this job.** A timer only lives in memory, and the runtime evicts idle objects well before an hour passes, so the flush is lost. Each request also starts its own timer, so 100 requests in an hour would mean 100 flushes for whichever timers survive. Use `setAlarm()` instead. It is saved to storage, wakes the object even after eviction, and is retried automatically if the handler throws.
3. **Calling `setAlarm()` on every request would push the flush back forever.** Each object has only one alarm, and `setAlarm()` replaces it. Under steady traffic the deadline would keep moving out and the alarm would never fire. Set it only when `getAlarm()` returns `null`.
4. **A sink failure counts as success, so it is never retried.** `fetch` only throws on network errors. A 4xx or 5xx response still returns normally, so `alarm()` completes and the runtime doesn't retry it. Throw when `!res.ok`.
5. **`pending` is never cleared, so every flush sends the same data again.** Nothing writes to `pending` either, so right now the body is `"null"`. Skip the POST when the list is empty.
6. **Clearing `pending` after the POST can drop entries.** While `await fetch(...)` is waiting, other requests can run and add to the list. Deleting the whole key afterwards would lose what they added. Remove only the entries you sent.
7. **The same batch can be delivered twice.** Alarms run at least once, so the handler can fire again after a POST that already succeeded. Send a batch id the sink can use to drop duplicates.
8. **Built-in retries eventually stop.** After the runtime gives up, nothing is scheduled until the next request. If that isn't acceptable, catch the error and call `setAlarm()` yourself with your own backoff.

```ts
import { DurableObject } from "cloudflare:workers";

const FLUSH_INTERVAL_MS = 60 * 60 * 1000;

export class Room extends DurableObject {
  async fetch(request: Request) {
    const count = (await this.ctx.storage.get<number>("count")) ?? 0;
    await this.ctx.storage.put("count", count + 1);
    // Append to "pending" here if that is what this request should record.

    if ((await this.ctx.storage.getAlarm()) === null) {
      await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + FLUSH_INTERVAL_MS);
    }
    return new Response(String(count + 1));
  }

  async alarm() {
    const batch = (await this.ctx.storage.get<string[]>("pending")) ?? [];
    if (batch.length === 0) return;

    const batchId = crypto.randomUUID();
    const res = await fetch("https://example.com/sink", {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "content-type": "application/json", "idempotency-key": batchId },
      body: JSON.stringify(batch),
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`sink returned ${res.status}`);

    // Remove only what was sent. Entries appended during the fetch stay queued.
    const current = (await this.ctx.storage.get<string[]>("pending")) ?? [];
    await this.ctx.storage.put("pending", current.slice(batch.length));
  }
}
```

The `slice` assumes new entries are only ever added to the end of `pending`. With a random id, a retry after a successful POST that failed before the trim gets a new id, so the sink can't catch that duplicate. If that matters, save the batch id in storage before the POST and reuse it on retry. For new classes the skill recommends SQLite storage (`new_sqlite_classes`), where `pending` would be a table you could delete from by id. The KV API above keeps your current storage backend.

I opened the durable-objects capability group and read its skill and rules reference, then reviewed the code against the alarm API. The group's tools only list or query deployed namespaces, so they couldn't check this source and I didn't call them.
